  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Upskilling Malaysian researchers in advanced physics skills via research into Shock-Boundary Layer Interaction Control in Hypersonic Flow using Hybrid Micro-Vortex Generators</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Capactiy building for Malaysia. Shock-wave turbulent boundary layer interaction (SWTBLI) interaction is a complex phenomenon that adversely affects the performance of supersonic flight vehicles. The micro-ramp is a recently developed novel passive flow control device that is a part of the micro-vortex generator (MVG) family. Although MVGs have shown potential in minimising the adverse effects of SWTBLI to a certain extent, previous studies indicate great scope for further improvement. In this investigation, the use of micro-ramps (passive flow control) will be paired with jet blowing (an active flow control mechanism).  SWTBLI involves complex physics and is challenging for investigation using experimental techniques and numerical simulations. In this project, we propose to study hybrid MVG&apos;s as the flow control device experimentally using the hypersonic wind tunnel facility at the University of Manchester, and numerically using large eddy simulation (LES) by STFC to obtain a better understanding of the flow physics. The primary aim of this activity is capacity building for Malaysia and Malaysian scientists, and as such the primary benefits are to Malaysia.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Newton Fund builds research and innovation partnerships with developing countries across the world to promote the economic development and social welfare of the partner countries.</narrative>
  </description>
